  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/medicine_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B016",
        "laureates": [
          "Douglas R. Lowy",
          "Ian H. Frazer",
          "John T. Schiller"
        ],
        "citation": "for virus-like-particle vaccines that prevent HPV infection and related cancers"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B027",
    "B004"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B016 most compellingly connects a defined biological and vaccine principle to mature, direct prevention of HPV infection and related cancers. Its preventive consequence is established at a population-relevant scale, and the listed trio remains a plausible focused attribution for the core VLP advance, provided the citation is kept to that advance rather than the full subsequent pathway of clinical development and implementation.",
    "case_against": "The allocation of credit in B016 remains its central weakness. Foundational virology, VLP design, clinical development, industrial work, and implementation are not interchangeable contributions, and the supplied evidence does not resolve their relative roles among Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller. B001 and B004 offer more compact two-person configurations, while B027 combines a narrowly defined mechanism with therapeutic benefit.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "linnarsson-sten",
        "point": "Linnarsson preferred B001 because its chaperonin mechanism and Horwich-Hartl attribution are more tightly delimited than the discovery-to-translation pathway in B016.",
        "response": "I agree that B001 has a real advantage in attributional compactness and that its citation can be framed with unusual precision. The decisive difference for me remains that B016 supports a specific preventive intervention with extensively realized cancer-prevention consequences. A narrowly restricted VLP citation can preserve that distinction without claiming credit for all later vaccine development or public-health implementation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "wahren-herlenius-marie",
        "point": "Wahren-Herlenius retained B004 because Miller and Cooper offer a focused account of the foundational B- and T-lymphocyte lineage discoveries, despite the broader historical development of lymphocyte biology.",
        "response": "I agree that B004 is a mature, foundational alternative with an exceptionally important biological framework. Its own need to selectively delimit a larger history shows that compact attribution alone cannot settle the comparison. I retain B016 because the defined VLP achievement has an unusually direct and durable preventive medical outcome, while recognizing that its historical allocation requires the same disciplined delimitation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "perlmann-thomas",
        "point": "Perlmann favored B009 for linking GLP-1 physiology to transformative therapies, while acknowledging uncertainty over the boundary between foundational biology and later drug development.",
        "response": "The comparison reinforces the need to distinguish an enabling discovery from the entire translational chain. B009 has extraordinary clinical importance, but its supplied evidence leaves the role of later therapeutic development and the fourth credited candidate unresolved. B016 also needs a narrow boundary, yet its proposed three-person set remains coherent for the VLP vaccine principle and its cancer-prevention outcome."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether the historical evidence can distinguish the respective experimental and translational contributions of Lowy, Frazer, and Schiller sufficiently to support all three names.",
      "Whether the VLP discovery can be cited narrowly enough to exclude later clinical, industrial, and implementation contributions while retaining the demonstrated preventive consequence.",
      "Whether the cleaner attribution of B001 or B004 should outweigh B016's more direct preventive medical impact."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile. My documented co-authorship connections concern Christopher Burge's group, Sten Linnarsson, Jonas Frisén, and Christoph Ziegenhain; no connection to the proposed B016 laureates is identified."
  }
}
